# Triage Decisions: Artifact Policy, Root-Cause Discipline & Routing

How the `nv-slang-bot[bot]` triage coworker classifies, routes, and posts on `shader-slang/slang` issues: GitHub artifact policy, root-cause verification, severity calls, cross-ref/dependency routing, and the coverage a recommended fix silently removes. Fixer PR creation/CI-fork mechanics are on [Fixer PR Creation, Fork & CI Mechanics](agent-fixer-pr-and-ci-mechanics.md); dispatch/coordination failure modes are on [Fixer Dispatch & Coordination Failure Modes](agent-fixer-dispatch-coordination.md).

## TL;DR

- **Post a verified 5-bullet on every triaged issue** on the bot's own authority. Exceptions: a non-draft PR carrying `Closes #N` *is* the artifact (a draft does NOT qualify — still post); a maintainer's own RFC with no novel value → stand down; dev-authored design/tracking issues → post as INPUT (solution-space, not a verdict), don't forward to fixer; a maintainer-authored tracking/planning placeholder → WATCH-ONLY (no comment, no label, no dispatch).
- **Check the last comment first** (double-post prevention); **refresh a posted verdict when someone acts on its text** (not merely when it goes stale).
- **Hold an interim classification 5-bullet until terminal**; author-facing scoping questions and missing-reproducer requests may post pre-terminal.
- **Resolve the reporter's release before root-causing** — `git tag --contains <fix-commit>` converts "fixed on master" into "shipped in vX.Y". A version-bearing report may describe an already-fixed defect and collapse to a version bump. A dependency-pin bump moves version + hash + every per-platform SHA256 together, or the release downloads UNVERIFIED.
- **Settle "would the fix even help?" with a same-image CI A/B, not a hypothesis** — grep the job log for `Image:` to prove a one-variable difference.
- **Empirically confirm root cause before fixing**; carry a hedge verbatim ("leading hypothesis (unconfirmed)", never "root cause (traced)"). Never pair a hunch with a suggested disposition — name the cheap measurement that settles it.
- **Treat any prior triage verdict (including your bot's) as a dated hypothesis to re-verify at HEAD**; when a measurement falsifies a premise, propagate the correction to every sibling issue resting on it.
- **Pointer-formation UB** (a `cur+N` pointer only *compared*, derefs short-circuit-guarded) is a **P2** UBSan finding, not a P1 crash.
- **Re-triage via the cross-ref timeline + per-PR `files`**, and `gh pr list --search` for an existing `Fixes #N` PR. An in-flight human/contributor PR, or a non-bot **assignee**, → routing decision to parent, never auto-dispatch the fixer.
- **A recommended path must name the coverage it REMOVES** — dropping a runtime read to keep a reflection/static check can gut a lowering/layout invariant reflection cannot see (reflection = frontend *prediction*, not proof lowering honored it).
- **Verify functional independence** before declaring a fix "stacks on" an unmerged PR (it often stands alone on master); a dependency-bump regression is proven by **cross-platform comparison**.

## GitHub Artifact Posting Policy

The canonical rule: post a verified 5-bullet issue comment on **every triaged issue** on the bot's own authority ([SUPERSEDED: 'skip GitHub post on dev-authored design placeholder' — post a deferential triage artifact anyway](wiki/learnings/1781137483321-superseded-skip-github-post-on-dev-authored-design.md), [Slang triage: labeling, Issue Type, and reproducer-request rules](wiki/learnings/1781008579138-slang-triage-labeling-issue-type-and-reproducer-re.md)). Superseded exceptions:

- **Fixed-via-PR exception** ([Triage + GitHub artifact: fixed-via-PR exception resolves the 'don't post interim verdict' vs 'artifact MUST land' tension](wiki/learnings/1780769170857-triage-github-artifact-fixed-via-pr-exception-reso.md)): when a non-draft PR already carries `Closes #N`, the PR *is* the artifact; triage need not duplicate it. A draft PR does NOT qualify — still post the issue comment.
- **Maintainer RFC stand-down** ([Triage: when to POST a 5-bullet vs STAND DOWN on a maintainer-authored design/RFC issue](wiki/learnings/1781266520028-triage-when-to-post-a-5-bullet-vs-stand-down-on-a-.md)): if it is the maintainer's own roadmap RFC (no bot mention, maintainer already driving a fix PR), AND there is no novel triage value to add → STAND DOWN (A2A disposition only; no issue comment). Otherwise post a deferential artifact.
- **Dev-authored design/tracking issues** ([SUPERSEDED: 'skip GitHub post on dev-authored design placeholder' — post a deferential triage artifact anyway](wiki/learnings/1781137483321-superseded-skip-github-post-on-dev-authored-design.md)): post anyway, but frame it as triage INPUT (solution-space mapping, not a verdict), defer the decision to the design owner, and do NOT forward to fixer.

**Interim verdict timing**: hold a classification 5-bullet ("fix incoming") until the chain reaches a terminal state; a bot comment that needs retraction costs credibility ([Hold interim triage classification 5-bullet until terminal; author-facing scoping questions are fine to post](wiki/learnings/1781116432142-hold-interim-triage-classification-5-bullet-until-.md)). Carve-out: author-facing scoping questions and missing-reproducer requests may post pre-terminal.

**Refresh a posted verdict when someone acts on its text.** The test is not *"is this detail stale?"* but *"does someone act on this text, and would the stale version mislead them?"* On #12325 a follow-up slang-rhi PR merged a stopgap whose TODOs named our issue as the re-enable trigger — with a trigger condition **already satisfied** — turning previously-cosmetic staleness into a live inaccuracy on an artifact a human would act on, which justified editing the posted comment in place. Mechanics: REST `PATCH` succeeded first try on the triager token while GraphQL has been 401 in recent sessions, so "try GraphQL on a 403" is not a dependable rescue rung ([refresh a posted verdict when someone acts on its text](wiki/learnings/1785765561758-check-the-reporter-s-version-before-root-causing-a.md)).

**Double-post prevention**: before posting any triage/resolution comment, check the last comment — `gh api repos/<owner>/<repo>/issues/<N>/comments --jq '.[-1] | "\(.user.login)\t\(.id)"'`. If `nv-slang-bot[bot]` already posted the current verdict, do NOT re-post ([Check for existing bot comment before posting GitHub triage artifact (cross-tier double-post risk)](wiki/learnings/1780768870271-check-for-existing-bot-comment-before-posting-gith.md)). "Always post" and "don't duplicate" are reconciled by *check-then-act*.

**Labeling and Issue Type** ([Slang triage: labeling, Issue Type, and reproducer-request rules](wiki/learnings/1781008579138-slang-triage-labeling-issue-type-and-reproducer-re.md)): apply `reproduced` only after reproducing on top-of-tree; if GPU/platform-dependent, skip both labels and note the limitation. Set Issue Type via GraphQL `updateIssue` — slang IDs: `Bug = IT_kwDOAb2kZs4AXYkt`, `Feature = IT_kwDOAb2kZs4AXYkw`. Human triage is authoritative — never change/remove a label a human set.

## Root-Cause Verification Discipline

**Before root-causing at HEAD, resolve which release the reporter is on.** A report naming a released version (`Slang version: 2026.12.2`) may describe a defect already fixed on master but not yet shipped. The check is four commands — `git grep '<fix-token>' <their-tag> -- source/` (absent at their tag?), `git grep '<fix-token>' -- source/` (present at HEAD?), `git log --format='%H' -S '<token>' -- <file> | tail -1` (introducing commit), then `git tag --contains <that-commit>`, which is the money command: it converts "fixed on master" into "shipped in vX.Y", the form that actually answers the reporter ([check whether the reporter's version predates the fix via git tag --contains](wiki/learnings/1785764482879-before-triaging-a-bug-report-check-whether-the-rep.md)). On slang#12325 this inverted the verdict: the `-std=metal4.0` producer already existed at master (`slang-code-gen.cpp:786-804` → `slang-gcc-compiler-util.cpp:971-988`, sharing the one `implies(metallib_4_0)` predicate with the `slang-emit-metal.cpp` emit gate), landed by PR #12009 (merge `a2596654f`, 07-15), first release **v2026.14**; the reporter was on 2026.12.2, where the consumer still hard-codes `-std=metal3.1` — deterministically their error. Both otherwise-obvious dispositions (close as a duplicate of #12096, or implement an already-implemented feature) were wrong: the issue collapsed to **bump the pinned Slang version in the consumer repo**, zero compiler work owed ([check the reporter's version before root-causing](wiki/learnings/1785765561758-check-the-reporter-s-version-before-root-causing-a.md)). Corollary — a version bump is rarely one line: slang-rhi's `SLANG_RHI_FETCH_SLANG_VERSION` (`CMakeLists.txt:148`) must move together with `SLANG_HASH_VERSION` (`:307`) and all **7** per-platform `SHA256` values, each wrapped in `if(SLANG_VERSION STREQUAL SLANG_HASH_VERSION)`; bump only the version and `URL_HASH` is left empty, so the release downloads **unverified**. Always check whether a pinned dependency carries hash/checksum state keyed to the old version.

**Settle "would the fix even help?" with a controlled comparison, not a hypothesis.** Find two CI jobs differing in exactly one variable and *prove* the rest matched: on #12325 a slang-rhi job (runner image `macos-26-arm64 / 20260728.0273`, fetched Slang 2026.12.2) gave `0 metal PASSED / 207 SKIPPED` with the metal4.0 error, while a same-day slang job on the **same image** with in-tree Slang gave **87 metal tests passed** — so `metal 32023.883` does accept `-std=metal4.0`, flipping the recommendation from "capability detection is the only real fix" to "the version bump fixes it, and capability detection is a separate hardening item." Grep the job log for `Image:` / `Image Release:` to prove the images match, otherwise it is not a controlled comparison; `gh api .../actions/jobs/<id>/logs` still works even when the run's own log archive has expired ([use CI logs as a controlled experiment on a one-variable difference](wiki/learnings/1785764482879-before-triaging-a-bug-report-check-whether-the-rep.md)).

Before implementing a fix, empirically confirm the root cause ([Verify triage root cause empirically — slang#10027 vector-default-N synthesized-ctor cross-module abort](wiki/learnings/1781724696064-verify-triage-root-cause-empirically-slang-10027-v.md)). A detailed, plausible triage trace can name the wrong inst or decl — especially for `InternalError: Generic type/value shouldn't be handled here!` aborts. The slang#10027 case: triage claimed a vector element *count* deserialized as `DeclRefIntVal(N)`, but an actual Debug backtrace showed the abort was on the element *type* param `T` from two un-reconciled copies of the synthesized `vector<T,4>` extension generic across the module boundary.

When a code-reader subagent hedges a hypothesis ("most likely", "NOT pinned down", "must be confirmed empirically"), carry that hedge verbatim into the public verdict — label it "leading hypothesis (unconfirmed)", never "root cause (traced)" ([Don't promote a hedged hypothesis to 'root cause (traced)' in a public triage verdict](wiki/learnings/1781724956224-don-t-promote-a-hedged-hypothesis-to-root-cause-tr.md)). The abort *mechanism* and guard line may be verified while the upstream *cause* remains a guess; split these in the public comment.

For parser-ambiguity triage involving "two-pass / pre-scan / decl-discovery" approaches, verify the **discovery step** separately from the resolution step ([Verify Discovery and Resolution Separately in Parser-Ambiguity Triage](wiki/learnings/1780064743810-verify-discovery-and-resolution-separately-in-pars.md)). The circularity often hides in the discovery pass: if the ambiguity is on member access (`Outer<…>.member<…>`), a top-level decl-name scan cannot resolve it without descending into bodies, which requires skipping `<…>` lists — the same problem at a different layer.

Before implementing, scan `/workspace/shared/learnings/INDEX.md` for prior triager rulings on the issue cluster ([A fresh unblock handoff can contradict a prior triager ruling — run Recall on shared learnings before implementing a multi-slice fix](wiki/learnings/1781775592867-a-fresh-unblock-handoff-can-contradict-a-prior-tri.md)). A "fresh unblock" handoff may contradict an earlier evidence-backed ruling about which slice owns a check. A test re-expression added solely to dodge a new check is a "do not mask" smell — stop and verify.

DeepWiki and same-day triage memos can describe an older codebase state ([Triage/DeepWiki concurrency premises can lag HEAD — verify mutex sites in source before accepting 'X is unsynchronized](wiki/learnings/1782408832985-triage-deepwiki-concurrency-premises-can-lag-head-.md)). On concurrency/thread-safety work, always `grep -rn` actual lock-acquisition sites at HEAD before accepting any "component X is unsynchronized" framing. **The same applies to a sibling issue's prior triage verdict — including your own bot's.** #12096's earlier bot triage concluded "no slang-core change is warranted" (arguing the macos-26 `metal` predates Metal 4); that was too strong — a slang-core change *was* warranted and landed as #12009, and the macos-15 pin (#12075) was reverted in #12129 precisely because it worked. Treat a prior triage comment as a **dated hypothesis to re-verify at HEAD**, not settled fact, especially when a newer issue's evidence contradicts it — and when a measurement falsifies it, propagate the correction to every other issue resting on the same premise rather than leaving it standing contradicted ([a stale triage verdict on the sibling issue can mislead you](wiki/learnings/1785764482879-before-triaging-a-bug-report-check-whether-the-rep.md), [propagate the correction to every issue resting on the falsified premise](wiki/learnings/1785765561758-check-the-reporter-s-version-before-root-causing-a.md)).

**Never ship a hunch with a suggested disposition (dispatch-side).** Anything an orchestrator asserts about the technical answer in a dispatch is read by the coworker as a **prior**, not an open question — and when that prior is wrong *and* points at a terminal disposition (duplicate / not-actionable / won't-fix), a competent coworker can execute flawlessly and still bury the real answer. The #12325 dispatch carried *"would passing `-std=metal4.0` even help against an Xcode-16-era toolchain that predates metal4.0 (probably not ⇒ real fix stays capability detection)… worth saying so politely"* — false, and had the triager accepted it the issue would have closed as a duplicate under a confidently-wrong rationale. Instead: mark technical hunches explicitly ("I suspect X; **treat as unverified**") and never pair one with a suggested disposition — the disposition is the investigator's to derive; name the **cheap measurement** that would settle it (a hunch with an attached experiment costs one job, a hunch with an attached conclusion costs a wrong close) ([never ship a hunch with a suggested disposition](wiki/learnings/1785765561758-check-the-reporter-s-version-before-root-causing-a.md)). This is the exported form of the invented-premise family: the bad premise lands in a subordinate's inputs, where it also acquires authority.

Triage memos verify the defect site deeply but cite surrounding code more loosely ([Verify triage-memo file:line claims about adjacent code before quoting in a PR](wiki/learnings/1782390307922-verify-triage-memo-file-line-claims-about-adjacent.md)). Before quoting any file:line claim about *adjacent* code (alternative-approach blast radius, "other callers do X") in a commit/PR/comment, grep/read it yourself.

## Severity Triage: Pointer-Formation UB Is Not Always a Realized Crash

When triaging a "memory-safety / UB pointer arithmetic" bug, distinguish **pointer formation** from **pointer dereference**. Forming a pointer more than one-past-the-end (e.g. `cur + 6 <= end` when fewer than 6 bytes remain) is UB per C++ `[expr.add]` and `-fsanitize=pointer-overflow` will flag it — but if that pointer is only *compared*, never *dereferenced*, there is no actual OOB read/write and no crash on mainstream flat-memory ISAs. Check whether the subsequent dereferences are short-circuit-guarded behind the bounds check (`&&` ordering); if they are, the realized severity is "latent UB / UBSan finding" (typically P2), NOT "crash/OOB" (P1). On slang #11864, `JSONStringEscapeHandler::appendUnescaped` formed `cur+6`/`cur+4` past-end pointers but all `cur[0]`/`_parseHex4(cur+2)` reads were guarded behind `cur+6 <= end` via `&&` — so the honest call was P2, not the recommended P1. A true P1 is when the pointer is dereferenced OOB or drives an unguarded memcpy/index. Fix idiom: compute `end - cur >= N` (always well-defined). Also pickaxe (`git log -S`) the exact site to attribute the introducing PR — sibling sites in one function can have different provenance (one a fresh regression, one latent for years) ([Pointer-formation UB (cur+N only compared, deref short-circuit-guarded) is UBSan-flaggable but NOT a realized crash — don't over-call P1](wiki/learnings/1782894644661-pointer-formation-ub-cur-n-only-compared-deref-sho.md)).

## Cross-Reference and Re-Triage Scanning

When re-triaging an issue, run the cross-reference timeline plus per-PR `files` check — not just a keyword search ([Re-triage: rescan live cross-ref timeline for newer maintainer PRs touching the issue's files](wiki/learnings/1781713625746-re-triage-rescan-live-cross-ref-timeline-for-newer.md)). A PR can address an issue's artifacts under another issue's `Closes #` link: `gh api repos/.../issues/<n>/timeline --jq '.[]|select(.event=="cross-referenced")...'`. When a maintainer's own PR already touches the files, do NOT auto-fire the fixer; surface the PR, recommend, and pose the design decision.

The same "don't auto-dispatch" caution extends to **issue ownership**: check `gh issue view <n> -R <repo> --json assignees --jq '[.assignees[].login]'` before any fixer handoff — a non-empty, non-bot assignee means a contributor owns it, so flag the ready-to-fix memo to Main/parent for authorization rather than dispatching (an ownership directive may live on an edge a group-scoped triager can't see, and dispatching risks a competing bot draft PR on a contributor's branch). Reaching `reproduced`/ready-to-fix does NOT release that ownership; the release trigger is the contributor un-assigning or an explicit "bot, take it" routed through Main ([triage handoff on a contributor-assigned issue: flag to parent first, don't auto-dispatch the fixer](wiki/learnings/1784793724947-triage-handoff-to-a-contributor-assigned-issue-fla.md)).

Always check for a linked fix PR before flagging an issue as untriaged in daily/maintainer reports ([Daily report: check for a linked fix PR before flagging an issue as untriaged](wiki/learnings/1781511232421-daily-report-check-for-a-linked-fix-pr-before-flag.md)). External contributors frequently file an issue and PR together; the issue may be unlabeled while the fix is deep in the human review pipeline.

Verify maintainer attributions in triage handoffs against `gh pr view <num> --json author,mergedBy,reviews` ([Verify maintainer attributions in triage handoffs against gh PR data](wiki/learnings/1780073122582-verify-maintainer-attributions-in-triage-handoffs-.md)). Bot triage comments have been observed asserting confident but wrong attribution (e.g. naming the wrong PR author). The actual coordination target is typically the issue assignee.

When a triage handoff names a contributor's existing PR as the fix, the triage outcome is **review/verify**, not implement from scratch ([slang: csyonghe files per-hunk issues AFTER opening the bundle PR — triage = verify, not implement](wiki/learnings/1780536698623-slang-csyonghe-files-per-hunk-issues-after-opening.md)). csyonghe's workflow: open a bundle PR first, then file per-sub-bug issues linking back. Forward to fixer framed as REVIEW.

## Dependency and Functional Independence

When a fix appears to depend on an unmerged sibling PR, flag it as "possible, fixer to confirm" — not a hard blocker ([slang triage: verify functional independence before declaring a fix 'stacks on' an unmerged PR — it often stands alone on master](wiki/learnings/1781186034425-slang-triage-verify-functional-independence-before.md)). Often the same outcome is achievable with APIs already in master; the shared helper is a convenience. Also check the sibling PR's actual diff size against master before recommending a stack — a long-lived fork can be thousands of files diverged.

For dependency-bump PRs (SPIRV-Tools/Headers, glslang, slang-rhi pin), the decisive test for "did the bump cause this?" is cross-platform comparison ([Dependency-bump PR triage: cross-platform check proves innocence](wiki/learnings/1782244083021-dependency-bump-pr-triage-cross-platform-check-pro.md)). A failure on only one platform/API while the same suite passes on siblings indicates a flake/infra issue, not a bump regression.

## Triage Recommendations: Audit the Coverage a Fix Removes

A triage recommendation that picks the fastest-looking fix can silently delete load-bearing coverage. On slang#12797 two descriptor-handle tests read an *unbound* bindless handle and masked the garbage with `* gOutput` (zero-init); the recommended **Approach A** — drop the unbound read, keep the `bindlessSpaceIndex` REFLECTION assertion, on the theory the tests traced to PR #9870 which is "about reflection" — was shipped, and the maintainer (jvepsalainen-nv, #9870's author) requested changes. Dropping the read removed not just the UB but the *live bindless heap*: a `DescriptorHandle<T>` declared-but-never-dereferenced emits no `GetDynamicResourceHeap`, so `lowerDynamicResourceHeap` early-returns and DCE strips `__slang_resource_heap` entirely (slang-ir-lower-dynamic-resource-heap.cpp:48-60). The tests existed to guard a *lowered descriptor-set placement* invariant (the heap lands in set 2 normally, set 3 when an unused ParameterBlock reserves a space first — a frontend pre-DCE reservation), and the reflection `bindlessSpaceIndex` check validates only the frontend *prediction*, not that lowering placed the heap there — so "keep reflection" kept the weak half and dropped the half that mattered. The maintainer-directed **Approach B** (already in the triage solution space, just not the recommendation) kept the read but made it *legal* — bind a real `RWStructuredBuffer<float>.Handle` via `TEST_INPUT` (buffer descriptor writes work on lavapipe; only IMAGE writes hit the suspected Mesa `VK_EXT_mutable_descriptor_type` bug) — plus a static `//CHECK: OpDecorate %__slang_resource_heap{{.*}}DescriptorSet N` to guard the lowered placement the reflection check cannot see. The reusable rule: before recommending "drop a runtime read + keep the reflection/static check" for a test that *looks* like it only hides a value (multiply-by-zero idiom), ask whether the runtime path is the ONLY thing exercising a lowering/codegen/layout invariant the reflection/JSON check cannot observe — and carry one line in every recommendation on *what coverage this fix removes, and whether that coverage is load-bearing* ([Triage: "drop the runtime read, keep the reflection assertion" can silently erase lowering/layout coverage](wiki/learnings/1788208011018-triage-drop-the-runtime-read-keep-the-reflection-a.md)).

## Resolution-Vehicle & Watch-Only Routing

**Triage dup-check must search for an existing `Fixes #N` PR.** The `gh` dup-check during triage MUST also `gh pr list -R <repo> --search "<issue#>" --state all` — the assignee may already own an in-flight `Fixes #N` PR. If one exists (especially a human's), do NOT reflexively forward to slang-fixer: that authors a competing PR against a live human-owned branch (double-dispatch). Surface it as a routing decision to the parent (park at triaged / help unblock / ping author) rather than defaulting to "forward to fixer — always." Case: slang#10584 had a stalled human draft PR #10666 (`Fixes #10584`, CI red, unaddressed review) — held forward, reported the routing decision up ([triage: gh-search for an existing Fixes-#N PR before forwarding to fixer](wiki/learnings/1784380560521-triage-gh-search-for-an-existing-fixes-n-pr-before.md)).

**Feature-issue vehicle search and folding a follow-up into its introducing PR.** Two triage-routing rules about resolution vehicles. When triaging an "add X support" feature issue, run `gh pr list --search "<keywords>" --state all` early — such issues frequently already have a stalled **bot-owned draft PR carrying `Fixes #N`** (e.g. #9038's takeover of #9085); the correct verdict is "resume/finish the existing PR" and, because a 2-week-old takeover may still own a live session, flag the parent for the routing call rather than unilaterally forwarding fresh work (a legitimate override of the always-forward-to-fixer default) ([triaging an add-X feature issue — search for an existing Fixes-#N PR before treating it as fresh work](wiki/learnings/1784739008680-triaging-an-add-x-support-feature-issue-search-for.md)). And when a docs/feature follow-up edits a file that **only an open, unmerged PR introduces** (verify with `git show origin/master:<path>`), the vehicle is to fold the new content into that PR with `Closes #<followup>`, NOT a rival PR off master that would conflict — and re-check the PR's draft/ready state at triage time, since a stale "draft" assumption changes the auto-close analysis ([a follow-up issue editing a file only introduced by an open PR must fold into that PR, not a rival PR off master](wiki/learnings/1784758259762-a-follow-up-issue-editing-a-file-only-introduced-b.md)).

**"Onboard-repo-to-dashboard" issues are ops/infra, not code triage.** A shader-slang issue titled like "Onboard repo to PR dashboard" (empty body, maintainer-authored, no repro) is an **operational/infrastructure** request about the coworker PR-dashboard / observability system — NOT a compiler/binding/docs task with a fix surface. Don't force a code triage or forward to a fixer: classify subsystem = infra/ops, post a brief triage note, and escalate to the orchestrator to own the dashboard side. Non-obvious fact that saves re-investigation (slangpy#1074): slangpy is likely *already* "onboarded" for the two common meanings — (a) the org PR-attention report (`slang-pr-report`) runs with `DEFAULT_REPOS=""`, surfacing open PRs for all non-archived shader-slang repos with no per-repo step, and (b) slangpy PRs already flow through the triage/fixer/reviewer/pr-approver pipeline as chain tiles. So ask the maintainer to disambiguate (a)/(b)/(c) a different-or-new dashboard + its end-state, rather than a generic "which dashboard?" ([Onboard-repo-to-dashboard issues are ops/infra, not code triage](wiki/learnings/1784913528281-onboard-repo-to-dashboard-issues-are-ops-infra-not.md)).

**Dependency-graph triage: doubly-gated re-enables, and maintainer-tracking placeholders.** Two triage shapes that need the full context resolved before a disposition. For a **"re-enable disabled tests once X merges" follow-up** (slangpy#1077), don't just check whether the *fix* PR merged — resolve the whole dependency graph: the `doctest::skip()` markers to remove are *added by* a separate disabling PR (#1076), so the issue is **doubly-gated** and needs BOTH #1076 merged (so skips exist to remove) AND the fix #1073 merged; if the fix merges but the disabling PR closes unmerged, the re-enable becomes moot. `gh pr view --json state,mergedAt` on *both*, and note that a fix PR at REVIEW_REQUIRED with green CI + zero reviews is blocked on a human approval, not code ([Test re-enable follow-ups can be doubly-gated (disabling PR + fix PR both unmerged)](wiki/learnings/1785162937774-test-re-enable-follow-ups-can-be-doubly-gated-disa.md)). And a **maintainer-authored tracking/planning placeholder** (slang#12241 "[Metal RayTracing] Start the implementation - part 1") is WATCH-ONLY / park-at-triaged: NO GitHub comment, NO label change, NO fixer dispatch — close the chain with an upstream `[Triage Resolution]` only. Signals: author is a self-filing+self-assigning core MEMBER/COLLABORATOR, Type/labels already human-set, body is a deferring umbrella placeholder, no reproducer expected. The "post a verified 5-bullet on every triaged issue" rule assumes there's a verdict a human needs; on the maintainer's own fully-triaged placeholder there's nothing to add, so a bot ack is noise. Still do the research to ground the report and catch a mis-shaped "tracking" issue that's actually a latent bug; RE-OPEN only on a fresh substantive human comment ([Maintainer-authored tracking/placeholder issue = watch-only, no GitHub post, no fixer](wiki/learnings/1785183281472-maintainer-authored-tracking-placeholder-issue-wat.md)).

## Contradictions / Supersessions

- The "skip GitHub post on dev-authored design placeholders" rule is RETIRED; post a deferential artifact for all reportable chain states ([SUPERSEDED: 'skip GitHub post on dev-authored design placeholder' — post a deferential triage artifact anyway](wiki/learnings/1781137483321-superseded-skip-github-post-on-dev-authored-design.md)).
- The "hold classification 5-bullet until terminal" rule ([Hold interim triage classification 5-bullet until terminal; author-facing scoping questions are fine to post](wiki/learnings/1781116432142-hold-interim-triage-classification-5-bullet-until-.md)) was later consolidated into the full posting policy; the fixed-via-PR exception ([Triage + GitHub artifact: fixed-via-PR exception resolves the 'don't post interim verdict' vs 'artifact MUST land' tension](wiki/learnings/1780769170857-triage-github-artifact-fixed-via-pr-exception-reso.md)) superseded the strict "terminal only" framing toward "verified verdict posts, unless a non-draft PR with Closes #N already covers it."
- The framing "a duplicate/dedup candidate named in the report is the likely disposition" is retired for version-bearing reports: #12325 carried a plausible dedup candidate (#12096) *and* a plausible alternative design, and both framings assumed a live slang-core defect that did not exist at the reporter's release. Run the `git tag --contains` release check **before** entertaining either ([check whether the reporter's version predates the fix](wiki/learnings/1785764482879-before-triaging-a-bug-report-check-whether-the-rep.md)). Relatedly, #12096's own "no slang-core change is warranted" line is SUPERSEDED — one was warranted and landed as #12009 ([propagate the falsified premise correction](wiki/learnings/1785765561758-check-the-reporter-s-version-before-root-causing-a.md)).

